<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as rock-hewn churches</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The rock-hewn churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> are a group of medieval churches carved directly out of rock in the town of Lalibela in the highlands of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. They are traditionally associated with King Lalibela of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> and are a major site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a> pilgrimage. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> cite them as part of the long Christian architectural tradition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> that followed Aksum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela.jpg" alt="Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalibela,_san_giorgio,_esterno_24.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The eleven rock-hewn churches stand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a> near the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>, named after King Gebre Meskel Lalibela of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a>, who is said to have commissioned them to recreate the holy city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in his own kingdom. According to local tradition, Lalibela, traditionally known as Roha, was founded by an Agew family called the Zagwa or Zagwe in 1137 CE. Tradition also holds that before he became king, Lalibela was guided by Christ on a tour of Jerusalem and told to build a second Jerusalem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><p>The king reigned about 1181 to 1221 and the work is said to have taken 24 years, though the churches more likely reached their present form over several phases of construction and alteration of earlier structures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The site lies at an altitude of around 2,480 metres. Five churches stand north of the town's river Jordan, five south of it, and one, Biete Giorgis, stands apart and is joined to the others by trenches. Tunnels and trenches connect the churches of each group. The northern churches are Biete Medhane Alem, Biete Maryam, Biete Golgotha Mikael, Biete Meskel and Biete Denagel, and the southern are Biete Amanuel, Biete Qeddus Mercoreus, Biete Abba Libanos, Biete Lehem and Biete Gabriel-Rufael.</p><p>The churches were made by taking material away. Workers used hammers and chisels to cut trenches round the monolithic and semi-monolithic structures out of scoriaceous basalt, working from the top down. Four are free-standing and seven share a wall with the mountain. Each is distinct, with human figures in bas-relief inside Bet Golgotha and coloured paintings of geometrical designs and biblical scenes in Bet Mariam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The churches are of great religious significance to Ethiopian Orthodox Christians, and their layout represents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The site is still used for daily worship and prayer, for festivals such as Timkat and Genna, and as a home for clergy, and it remains a place of pilgrimage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> first placed it on the World Heritage List in 1978. In 2008 four shelters were put up to cover five of the churches as a temporary protection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zagwe dynasty, which succeeded Aksum, built the famous <b>rock-hewn churches</b> of Lalibela in a style showing clear Aksumite architectural influence, so Aksum's culture survived its political collapse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
